$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 12/Practice
::HC-W12-P01::An HIV campaign uses images implying people with HIV are dangerous to frighten audiences into testing. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=stigma-producing fear appeal#Correct. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
~privacy-invasive targeting#Not the best answer. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
~equity harm through channel substitution#Not the best answer. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
~unsafe AI hallucination#Not the best answer. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
}

::HC-W12-P02::An HIV campaign uses images implying people with HIV are dangerous to frighten audiences into testing. What is the strongest next response? {
=Replace stigmatising imagery with accurate risk information, efficacy and respectful testing guidance.#Correct. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
~Use consented data and avoid sensitive inference that users would not reasonably expect.#Not the best answer. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
~Retain or strengthen accessible alternatives and monitor outcomes by subgroup.#Not the best answer. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
~Constrain the system, validate clinical content, monitor errors and route high-risk questions to qualified humans.#Not the best answer. Short-term attention does not justify harm to affected people.
}

::HC-W12-P03::A personalised fertility campaign infers pregnancy status from browsing data without explicit consent. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=privacy-invasive targeting#Correct. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
~equity harm through channel substitution#Not the best answer. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
~unsafe AI hallucination#Not the best answer. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
~deceptive synthetic media#Not the best answer. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
}

::HC-W12-P04::A personalised fertility campaign infers pregnancy status from browsing data without explicit consent. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use consented data and avoid sensitive inference that users would not reasonably expect.#Correct. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
~Retain or strengthen accessible alternatives and monitor outcomes by subgroup.#Not the best answer. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
~Constrain the system, validate clinical content, monitor errors and route high-risk questions to qualified humans.#Not the best answer. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
~Disclose that the representation is synthetic or use authentic consented testimony.#Not the best answer. Personalisation can become surveillance when sensitive status is inferred covertly.
}

::HC-W12-P05::A digital service improves access for urban smartphone users while rural alternatives are discontinued. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=equity harm through channel substitution#Correct. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
~unsafe AI hallucination#Not the best answer. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
~deceptive synthetic media#Not the best answer. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
~manipulative risk communication#Not the best answer. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
}

::HC-W12-P06::A digital service improves access for urban smartphone users while rural alternatives are discontinued. What is the strongest next response? {
=Retain or strengthen accessible alternatives and monitor outcomes by subgroup.#Correct. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
~Constrain the system, validate clinical content, monitor errors and route high-risk questions to qualified humans.#Not the best answer. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
~Disclose that the representation is synthetic or use authentic consented testimony.#Not the best answer. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
~Present benefits, harms and uncertainty accurately while making the recommended action clear.#Not the best answer. Average improvement can hide widening inequity.
}

::HC-W12-P07::An AI chatbot invents a contraindication while sounding confident. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unsafe AI hallucination#Correct. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
~deceptive synthetic media#Not the best answer. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
~manipulative risk communication#Not the best answer. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
~data-retention and consent problem#Not the best answer. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
}

::HC-W12-P08::An AI chatbot invents a contraindication while sounding confident. What is the strongest next response? {
=Constrain the system, validate clinical content, monitor errors and route high-risk questions to qualified humans.#Correct. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
~Disclose that the representation is synthetic or use authentic consented testimony.#Not the best answer. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
~Present benefits, harms and uncertainty accurately while making the recommended action clear.#Not the best answer. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
~Minimise retained identifiers, define retention and obtain consent for each intended use.#Not the best answer. Fluency is not evidence of clinical accuracy.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 12/Mastery
::HC-W12-M01::A campaign uses a synthetic image presented as a real survivor testimony. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=deceptive synthetic media#Correct. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
~manipulative risk communication#Not the best answer. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
~data-retention and consent problem#Not the best answer. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
~potential discriminatory targeting#Not the best answer. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
}

::HC-W12-M02::A campaign uses a synthetic image presented as a real survivor testimony. What is the strongest next response? {
=Disclose that the representation is synthetic or use authentic consented testimony.#Correct. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
~Present benefits, harms and uncertainty accurately while making the recommended action clear.#Not the best answer. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
~Minimise retained identifiers, define retention and obtain consent for each intended use.#Not the best answer. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
~Audit model inputs, burden, benefit and fairness before deployment and provide transparent safeguards.#Not the best answer. Viewers may reasonably infer a real person and lived experience.
}

::HC-W12-M03::A message exaggerates certainty about a screening benefit because staff believe people will otherwise not attend. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=manipulative risk communication#Correct. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
~data-retention and consent problem#Not the best answer. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
~potential discriminatory targeting#Not the best answer. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
~threat without efficacy and support#Not the best answer. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
}

::HC-W12-M04::A message exaggerates certainty about a screening benefit because staff believe people will otherwise not attend. What is the strongest next response? {
=Present benefits, harms and uncertainty accurately while making the recommended action clear.#Correct. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
~Minimise retained identifiers, define retention and obtain consent for each intended use.#Not the best answer. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
~Audit model inputs, burden, benefit and fairness before deployment and provide transparent safeguards.#Not the best answer. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
~Pair proportionate risk information with clear, accessible cessation options and support.#Not the best answer. Public-health goals do not justify material deception.
}

::HC-W12-M05::A programme collects community stories for a campaign and stores names indefinitely though the stories are published anonymously. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=data-retention and consent problem#Correct. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
~potential discriminatory targeting#Not the best answer. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
~threat without efficacy and support#Not the best answer. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
~unverified automated translation#Not the best answer. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
}

::HC-W12-M06::A programme collects community stories for a campaign and stores names indefinitely though the stories are published anonymously. What is the strongest next response? {
=Minimise retained identifiers, define retention and obtain consent for each intended use.#Correct. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
~Audit model inputs, burden, benefit and fairness before deployment and provide transparent safeguards.#Not the best answer. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
~Pair proportionate risk information with clear, accessible cessation options and support.#Not the best answer. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
~Require competent human review and audience testing before publication.#Not the best answer. Anonymised publication does not remove backend privacy risk.
}

::HC-W12-M07::An algorithm sends obesity messages more aggressively to one ethnic group based on predicted risk. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=potential discriminatory targeting#Correct. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
~threat without efficacy and support#Not the best answer. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
~unverified automated translation#Not the best answer. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
~average outcome masking inequity#Not the best answer. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
}

::HC-W12-M08::An algorithm sends obesity messages more aggressively to one ethnic group based on predicted risk. What is the strongest next response? {
=Audit model inputs, burden, benefit and fairness before deployment and provide transparent safeguards.#Correct. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
~Pair proportionate risk information with clear, accessible cessation options and support.#Not the best answer. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
~Require competent human review and audience testing before publication.#Not the best answer. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
~Disaggregate meaningful outcomes and redesign inaccessible components.#Not the best answer. Risk targeting can reproduce bias and stigma.
}

::HC-W12-M09::A smoking campaign uses highly graphic fear imagery but provides no cessation support. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=threat without efficacy and support#Correct. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
~unverified automated translation#Not the best answer. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
~average outcome masking inequity#Not the best answer. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
~lack of transparency about automation#Not the best answer. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
}

::HC-W12-M10::A smoking campaign uses highly graphic fear imagery but provides no cessation support. What is the strongest next response? {
=Pair proportionate risk information with clear, accessible cessation options and support.#Correct. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
~Require competent human review and audience testing before publication.#Not the best answer. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
~Disaggregate meaningful outcomes and redesign inaccessible components.#Not the best answer. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
~Clearly disclose the automated nature, limitations and route to human help.#Not the best answer. Fear without feasible action can produce avoidance or distress.
}

::HC-W12-M11::A translation tool generates health messages in a minority language that no fluent reviewer checks. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=unverified automated translation#Correct. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
~average outcome masking inequity#Not the best answer. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
~lack of transparency about automation#Not the best answer. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
~ethical blind spot in formative testing#Not the best answer. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
}

::HC-W12-M12::A translation tool generates health messages in a minority language that no fluent reviewer checks. What is the strongest next response? {
=Require competent human review and audience testing before publication.#Correct. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
~Disaggregate meaningful outcomes and redesign inaccessible components.#Not the best answer. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
~Clearly disclose the automated nature, limitations and route to human help.#Not the best answer. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
~Include affected groups safely and assess stigma, dignity and unintended interpretation before launch.#Not the best answer. Translation errors can alter medical meaning and trust.
}

::HC-W12-M13::A programme reports only overall campaign success despite much lower comprehension among people with disabilities. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=average outcome masking inequity#Correct. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
~lack of transparency about automation#Not the best answer. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
~ethical blind spot in formative testing#Not the best answer. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
~benefit-harm trade-off with privacy breach#Not the best answer. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
}

::HC-W12-M14::A programme reports only overall campaign success despite much lower comprehension among people with disabilities. What is the strongest next response? {
=Disaggregate meaningful outcomes and redesign inaccessible components.#Correct. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
~Clearly disclose the automated nature, limitations and route to human help.#Not the best answer. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
~Include affected groups safely and assess stigma, dignity and unintended interpretation before launch.#Not the best answer. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
~Redesign notification consent and privacy protections before claiming the programme is ethically successful.#Not the best answer. Equity requires examining who benefits, not only the mean.
}

::HC-W12-M15::A chatbot does not tell users that they are interacting with an automated system. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=lack of transparency about automation#Correct. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
~ethical blind spot in formative testing#Not the best answer. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
~benefit-harm trade-off with privacy breach#Not the best answer. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
~checklist substituted for ethical judgement#Not the best answer. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
}

::HC-W12-M16::A chatbot does not tell users that they are interacting with an automated system. What is the strongest next response? {
=Clearly disclose the automated nature, limitations and route to human help.#Correct. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
~Include affected groups safely and assess stigma, dignity and unintended interpretation before launch.#Not the best answer. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
~Redesign notification consent and privacy protections before claiming the programme is ethically successful.#Not the best answer. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
~Use SIGNAL as an integration aid while conducting separate ethical, privacy and equity review.#Not the best answer. Users need information to calibrate trust and expectations.
}
$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Health Communication/Week 12/Institutional
::HC-W12-I01::A campaign pre-test excludes the minority group most likely to be stigmatised by the message. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=ethical blind spot in formative testing#Correct. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
~benefit-harm trade-off with privacy breach#Not the best answer. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
~checklist substituted for ethical judgement#Not the best answer. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
~stigma-producing fear appeal#Not the best answer. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
}

::HC-W12-I02::A campaign pre-test excludes the minority group most likely to be stigmatised by the message. What is the strongest next response? {
=Include affected groups safely and assess stigma, dignity and unintended interpretation before launch.#Correct. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
~Redesign notification consent and privacy protections before claiming the programme is ethically successful.#Not the best answer. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
~Use SIGNAL as an integration aid while conducting separate ethical, privacy and equity review.#Not the best answer. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
~Replace stigmatising imagery with accurate risk information, efficacy and respectful testing guidance.#Not the best answer. Harms may be invisible to designers and majority audiences.
}

::HC-W12-I03::A communication intervention improves clinic attendance but exposes some adolescents’ service use to parents through shared-phone notifications. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=benefit-harm trade-off with privacy breach#Correct. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
~checklist substituted for ethical judgement#Not the best answer. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
~stigma-producing fear appeal#Not the best answer. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
~privacy-invasive targeting#Not the best answer. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
}

::HC-W12-I04::A communication intervention improves clinic attendance but exposes some adolescents’ service use to parents through shared-phone notifications. What is the strongest next response? {
=Redesign notification consent and privacy protections before claiming the programme is ethically successful.#Correct. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
~Use SIGNAL as an integration aid while conducting separate ethical, privacy and equity review.#Not the best answer. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
~Replace stigmatising imagery with accurate risk information, efficacy and respectful testing guidance.#Not the best answer. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
~Use consented data and avoid sensitive inference that users would not reasonably expect.#Not the best answer. Positive average outcomes do not erase serious subgroup harms.
}

::HC-W12-I05::A team uses the LIPPS SIGNAL checklist and concludes ethical review is unnecessary because every SIGNAL element is complete. Which diagnosis or concept best identifies the main communication issue? {
=checklist substituted for ethical judgement#Correct. A planning checklist cannot replace formal judgement about rights and harms.
~stigma-producing fear appeal#Not the best answer. A planning checklist cannot replace formal judgement about rights and harms.
~privacy-invasive targeting#Not the best answer. A planning checklist cannot replace formal judgement about rights and harms.
~equity harm through channel substitution#Not the best answer. A planning checklist cannot replace formal judgement about rights and harms.
}

::HC-W12-I06::A team uses the LIPPS SIGNAL checklist and concludes ethical review is unnecessary because every SIGNAL element is complete. What is the strongest next response? {
=Use SIGNAL as an integration aid while conducting separate ethical, privacy and equity review.#Correct. A planning checklist cannot replace formal judgement about rights and harms.
~Replace stigmatising imagery with accurate risk information, efficacy and respectful testing guidance.#Not the best answer. A planning checklist cannot replace formal judgement about rights and harms.
~Use consented data and avoid sensitive inference that users would not reasonably expect.#Not the best answer. A planning checklist cannot replace formal judgement about rights and harms.
